          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-tenant-of-wildfell-hall-0e20a0f36fe0556a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":2,\"performative\":4,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Mr. Boarham’s conditional “I would love you” partly presents prospective love as genuine, but its dominant force is an offer or undertaking within his marriage proposal: if accepted, he would love, cherish, and protect Helen. The deliberate, argumentative courtship context supplies no independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The modal “would” leaves some uncertainty between a prediction or avowal of prospective feeling (T) and a conditional marital undertaking (P), though the coordinated promises strongly favor P.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Mr. Boarham’s spoken words are quoted by Helen in her retrospective written narration; the represented original event is a direct courtship utterance.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Boarham says this while persistently pressing a marriage proposal after Helen has clearly rejected him. Coordinating “love” with “cherish” and “protect” casts it as part of a prospective husband’s undertaking and persuasive appeal. Helen’s narration portrays his paternalism and persistence critically, but that framing affects the proposal’s reception rather than displacing its T/P core force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“But why so? I would love you, cherish you, protect you”\",\"supports\":\"The modal construction and coordinated prospective acts strongly support a conditional relational undertaking (P), with some support for presenting future love as genuine (T).\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Boarham argues for marriage and asks, “since I am satisfied, why should you object,” after describing Helen as his chosen prospective wife.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes the utterance as part of a considered marriage proposal rather than an affect-driven verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Helen says he “pertinaciously interrupted” her and later kept “repeating the same arguments over and over again.”\",\"supports\":\"Supports an argumentative and calculated persuasive delivery, with no positive evidence for expressive/reflexive E force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Helen finally tells him, “I cannot love you, and never could,” and asks him to stop.\",\"supports\":\"Shows that his promised love is unwanted and coercively persistent in context, without creating a residual core-force category beyond T/P/E.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is annotated as affirmative modal form with the exact match “I would love you.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the conditional/modal grammatical framing relevant to the balance between prospective assertion and undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and especially P naturally capture the prospective avowal and conditional marital undertaking. The narrator’s critical framing and Boarham’s pressure are contextual features, while no independent E or residual O force is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
